# edit — caps.decl
#
# Wave: R71.M1-001 (edit#1), bootstrap.
# Doc:  design/roadmap/post-r60-daily-use-roadmap.md §"R71 -- vi-like
#       TUI editor `edit`" in paideia-os.
#
# Purpose
# -------
# This file is the invariant-I6 (capability handoff visible +
# refusable) manifest the shell validates at exec: edit receives
# exactly the caps below and no more. Missing a required cap in the
# reconciled set is an exec refusal; extra caps in the received set
# are dropped at cap_manifest_verify (libpdx-cap M2-001) before
# edit's `_start` observes them. This is the R71.M1 bootstrap shape
# — the minimum surface edit#1..edit#5 need — not yet reconciled
# against the R90-XREPO adoption wave the more mature coreutil repos
# (cat, rm, mv, mkdir) have already gone through; that reconciliation
# is future work once edit reaches a comparable milestone.
#
# requires:
#
#   KIND_USER
#     Identity root. No direct use at R71.M1 (no per-user audit trail
#     yet); declared per this org's baseline convention (every
#     shipping tool declares KIND_USER even before it has a concrete
#     consumer) so a future audit/undo integration does not need a
#     caps.decl edit to add it.
#
#   KIND_TTY (write)
#     Every byte edit writes goes to fd 1 as a raw ANSI/CSI sequence
#     via plain sys_write (TtyRaw::edit_tty_raw_enter/exit,
#     tty_raw_cursor_to, tty_raw_erase_line; Render::render_frame's
#     content writes). No KIND_TTY(read) is declared here: edit#2's
#     scope is alt-screen + cursor addressing over ordinary fd-1
#     writes, NOT the ICANON/ECHO raw-mode toggle that would require
#     a KIND_TTY row + TTY_OP_SET_RAW cap_invoke -- that substrate
#     gap is still open even for shell's own line editor (see
#     shell/src/line_reader.pdx's lr_tty_set_raw / lr_tty_set_cooked,
#     both scaffolded no-ops) and is out of scope for this cohort.
#     Keystroke input (Editor::_start's edit_main_loop) reads fd 0
#     via the ordinary sys_read SC+ ID, not a capability-gated path.
#
#   KIND_PDXFS_FILE (read)
#   KIND_PDXFS_FILE (write)
#     File open+read (initial load from argv[1], Ctrl+O / `:e <path>`
#     reload) and open+write (Ctrl+S / `:w` save). UNNARROWED as of
#     edit#6 (R71.M1-006): `:e <path>` lets the user retarget the
#     editor's working file to ANY path typed at the command line at
#     runtime, not just argv[1] -- an editor's whole point is opening
#     arbitrary files a session decides on interactively, so (unlike
#     cat/cp's single-argv-path narrowing, which fits a one-shot batch
#     tool) an `<arg-path>` narrowing would have to be re-validated on
#     every `:e`, which no cap-narrowing primitive in this codebase
#     supports mid-process today. This is the same posture a real
#     shell/editor pair takes: the interactive tool holds a broad file
#     capability and the shell's own exec-time reconciliation (this
#     manifest) is the actual gate on which BINARIES may hold it, not
#     which paths a already-running one may touch. Revisit if/when a
#     mid-process cap-renarrowing primitive lands.
#
#   KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT
#     Reserved for a future semantic-pipe emission (an EditRecord@0.1
#     on save, mirroring cat's CatPipeRecord@0.1 / cp's CopyRecord
#     @0.1 precedent) and/or a future libpdx-audit hook on save.
#     Declared now, not yet invoked -- same DECLARED-but-not-INVOKED
#     posture rm#28's KIND_PDXFS_VOL row documents (the manifest
#     reconciler treats a declared-but-unused cap as informational,
#     not CAP_MANIFEST_EXTRA).
#
# Missing caps in the received set -> exit path TBD (edit has no
# errno-mapped diagnostic layer yet at R71.M1; that is future polish
# alongside the ex-command line at edit#6).

requires:
  - KIND_USER
  - KIND_TTY (write)
  - KIND_PDXFS_FILE (read)
  - KIND_PDXFS_FILE (write)
  - KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT

# No declared output schemas at R71.M1 -- the semantic-pipe emission
# noted above is reserved, not yet wired.
